/** siman 467 part 2 — Pesach */
export const fixes = {
  "output/siman_467/chok-yaakov/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א": "Or that fell upon them. And this is the language of the Ran in chapter Kol Sha'ah: even if they split open such that the chametzing is recognizable in them, nevertheless it is forbidden to sell to a gentile, for we are concerned lest he grind them and afterward sell to an Israelite — end of his words. However the Tur wrote and the chametz is not recognizable in them — it implies at first glance that if the chametz is recognizable in them we are not concerned at all, and it is difficult, for there is still the concern of the Ran. And similarly sensed this difficulty on the Tur in Olat Shabbat — see there — and Magen Avraham answered and this is its language: we are not concerned lest he grind them and sell them to an Israelite since one does not buy flour from the market except in a time of distress as written in siman 453, unlike Olat Shabbat — end of his words. But in truth he did not correct anything with this answer, for that one does not buy except in a time of distress means specifically for matzah of the commandment as written above siman 453 note 15, and even for us who are accustomed to perform guarding for all the matzos — nevertheless when an Israelite buys that flour before Pesach and retains it in his house he transgresses \"it shall not be seen,\" since the chametz was recognizable in them — and this is plainly the opinion of the Ran. Therefore it appears to me the main intent of the Tur in what he wrote and the chametz is not recognizable is for amplification: even though the chametz is not recognizable and only a general concern of chametzing is known in it (as the Tur wrote explicitly), nevertheless we are concerned for mishap in the matter of selling, and all the more so when chametz is known and recognizable with certainty we are concerned for mishap — and it is clear:",
    "1:ב": "Forbidden to retain it. If he transgressed and retained it and sowed it in the ground and growths sprouted, the growths are permitted as written above siman 453:",
    "1:ג": "And informs. So is the language of the Rambam, and the Tur challenged him, for since our Talmud's version is \"we sell it keva keva to an Israelite\" so that it finishes before Pesach, and since it is permitted only a small amount — if he informs, even much is also permitted — see in Beit Yosef and other Acharonim how they reconcile the words of the Rambam and the Tur, and through this they raised several laws; and in truth this question was already asked before the Rashba in responsum siman 254, and he answered that it deals with informing and there is no difficulty even for much as well, since an Israelite does not buy much since it is forbidden to retain it on Pesach (the opposite of my purpose — why should he be forced to sell to a gentile little by little near Pesach, for a gentile is found at all times in every place who will buy from him and return to him after Pesach as stated above siman 448 — see there), and as we say in Nedarim that an Israelite does not benefit from him — he buys for less and sells for more — only they do not listen to him; the same here — end of his words. It is found that the words of the Rambam are correct without doubt, and one need not corrupt the versions before us as the Maggid Mishneh wrote, and what astonished Beit Yosef and other Acharonim that escaped them the words of the Rashba; and so is the essence that when he informs, even much is permitted to sell to an Israelite, unlike the implication of some Acharonim; and even little by little, if he sells to an Israelite he must inform — and so agreed the Acharonim:",
    "1:ד": "(4) so that he could before Passover. P. even the ETL model which the little we will sell to Israel MM the little Israel will eat before Pesach and the PZ means DBA'P at the fifth hour in the matter that it is impossible that before Pesach Aso' could sell to the ACM even a little bit for fear that Dawley will sell to Israel and also the meaning of the language of the Shas and Rambam and other arbiters even per the HMM according to his version per the Rambam in Shas Dilan as well as the agreement of the MA Dela Km and also the meaning of the author Nan. And from M.A.L.A.D., we will leave Israel with a large amount of grain, and it is in the matter that if he sells it to an ACM who could before Passover and if there is a large loss, it is permissible to sell it to an ACM who knows about it and knows that he will not touch it at all, but we will keep it for him until after Pesach and you may say to him until you take K. A little bit is forbidden in the rest of the forbidden, only this one, Gabi chametz, that one, a lot of sufficiency, his words are true, and since in this to the Mash, also in the books of MI Klal M6, Sec.",
    "1:ה": "And he sells it to an Israelite. And if the gentile said to the Israelite even a word after he sold him wheat that these wheats are for sweets — it is like what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 216 that a gentile is not believed in what he said after he sold them and they left his possession; however if he is believed, etc., it is forbidden — end quote — Magen Avraham. It implies before he sold them, since it is in his possession he is believed — and this is not so, for since one can say he intended to enhance his merchandise he is not believed as stated in Yevamos page 22, and so in Yoreh De'ah siman 116, and Shach and Taz, and so I raised in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 59 note 7 — see there; and it is possible this is not enhancement of the merchandise, for on the contrary greater enhancement is to wheat if they are not yet for sweets and are clean in themselves — therefore he is believed if it is in his possession — uncertain:",
    "2:א": "This grain that was submerged in a river. And whether it is permitted to nullify this grain in majority or in sixty before Pesach, since it is still a time of permission — the Acharonim expanded on this, and see above siman 442 note 18 and siman 467 note 15 what emerges from their words for practical halachah:",
    "2:ב": "(2) Dachion Deminach will put it in the hand of Himmutz as the Chief Rabbi and the Torah in the name of Rabbi Ephraim and according to what the Torah and the Rabbi ruled in all cloves for strawberries even if they do not kicho dalmichal hetta in his eyes Efi' they were not really cracked but those close to the cleft are also prohibited and so on. When Moel debainan is actually torn apart, if it is possible, it is only severe on one side, and in their eyes, it is close to being torn apart. And the author Sal Dalmichal, in his eyes, doubted it and made it worse, as explained in section 9. Therefore, the ruling here is that Deminach Neicho has not softened and is not close to being torn apart. And this is also prohibited The fact that the author, etc., has been careful here, even if it has not been broken, is simply forbidden. And not 20, as l'fi da'at, section 9 of Efi', we do not really break down, but to show that the chametz is not close to breaking down, because the container of the chametz is prohibited here in his eyes, but if it is present in the stew, the author ruled that l'fi da'at, section 9, does not really break down. A chametz per the column C. 1951 and 1667 YAS, only that it must be felt that it was in the water before boiling, therefore it does not chametz all of the Hai and Amash above in C. 1914 and above and it is the main part of a stew that is only a mixture of something spurned, not all of the Island chametz as the 18th chapter of the chapter 12th: and per the Rambam's MSS in the P.H. Or we will cook and not break down really is not a chametz of The Torah and also something d'rabbanan did not prohibit the Torah d'rabbanan and the 23rd p. L.M. there the words of the Rambam and in this way the words of the Rif to the Rambam without doubt and Dela the 22nd which strongly suppresses the intent of the Rambam's words in this according to this reason we will interfere Dagan is a species of its kind Efi' in less number is only a d'rabbanan prohibition also in the opinion of the author it should be lightened and I extended all this to the opinion of the judges and the author For valid reasons, but for the sake of the law, Rama already concludes in Sai' 9 of the Haga, that it is customary to prohibit in every matter and Ams there. And it is clear from the words of the author in section A and this section that Dafi' were not broken, it is forbidden to sell to the Akoum before Pesach, but they are sold little by little, as in Mordechai P.",
    "2:ג": "They come to chametzing. And if it has not yet waited the measure of a mil, as Magen Avraham wrote — even if it occurred on Pesach it is permitted to sell it to a gentile before it chametzs:",
    "2:ד": "But not to eat them on Pesach it is forbidden. Even if some of them were eaten before Pesach, one may not permit eating the rest as Bach concludes here, unlike Ravyah who specifically dry with dry and one of them was eaten, or in three mixtures one may permit eating two as explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 110 — unlike moist with moist there is no permission in this, and so Magen Avraham; (and see above siman 453 where it was explained that flour in flour is called moist with moist) and so I also raised in my pamphlet of uncertainties note 19, unlike Shach — see there:",
    "2:ה": "And a double uncertainty does not help. In truth in this seif and in several places it is explained that we rely on double uncertainty even in eating — see Taz and Shach who wrote the reason: since according to Mar Ukva even if they did not split open it is forbidden, it is only a double uncertainty of dispute and not in the reality of the matter — we do not lenient so much in such an uncertainty; and see in my pamphlet of uncertainties newly 2 note 2 where it was explained that on the contrary, uncertainty of dispute is better than uncertainty of the act itself; and also one cannot say that therefore the Rav was stringent in such an uncertainty because all are one uncertainty whether these wheats chametzed from the water or not — I already rejected this reasoning too in pamphlet of uncertainties siman 120, and see there what the Rav wrote many reasons on this, and the essence appears to me like the conclusion of the Taz that here for this reason they were stringent because there is no loss in retaining it — but in truth in a place of loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy and the like one may permit even in eating:",
    "3:א": "Dry and hard. However if water came upon them one should forbid the wheat kernel itself even though it did not change at all, as written in note 7, and even in cordovan wheat as stated in Pesachim in the case of Rav Gidal — see there in Rashi:",
    "3:ב": "And their appearance did not change. But if their appearance changed or they are moist, it is forbidden even to retain them — Tur in the name of BeHag:",
    "4:_": "We are not concerned for ordinary wheat. And Knesset HaGedolah wrote in practical halachah that he permitted buying wheat for Pesach even though they were stored in pits, all that we did not see in them a change in appearance and they are not moist — see there where he expanded, and if there are among them wheats that sprouted — see above siman 453:",
    "6:_": "The upper part of the stack and its lower part. Rokeach wrote in siman 263 and this is its language: and if a little dew or water and snow on his grain, he scrapes those wheat kernels and takes what is beneath them as stated in chapter Kol Sha'ah in Yerushalmi — end of his words:",
    "7:_": "Split wheat. But if they are not split open one should not be stringent here at all, for even split open there is no complete chametzing here, only spoilage and loss, as the Rashba wrote in responsum, and see above siman 476 and what is written, and siman 466 from the law of sweat of heat:",
    "8:א": "We do not establish prohibition on it to say it is forged. So is the language of the Tur, and it implies that if we know flour is mixed in it we must burn as stated explicitly in Yerushalmi end of chapter Kol Sha'ah, and one may not rely on the permission the Rosh wrote in the name of R' Yonah, and this is its language: and if we come to forbid the honey because of concern for mixture, that honey is forbidden all year, for they put nevelah meat into it and it transforms into honey — and R' Yonah wrote that even if they put nevelah meat into it, since it dissolved and became honey it is permitted, for we follow the present — end of his words. From this, according to his words, even if we know with certainty flour is mixed in it one may permit it; nevertheless one may not rely on this permission against the Yerushalmi who are stringent against R' Yonah's opinion, for the Rosh himself in chapter Keitzad Mevarchin doubts this view of R' Yonah, and also in Knesset HaGedolah he wrote one may not rely on this permission lest it returns and revives during Pesach — see there where he expanded; nevertheless it appears to me all this is if we know with certainty flour was mixed into it — but from the ordinary, even though they are accustomed to forbid eating them in their own form, nevertheless if it became mixed in a cooked dish one may permit b'dieved even in less than sixty, or even if we know flour was mixed into it it is permitted to retain in his house, or if it became mixed in a cooked dish if there is great loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy, even in less than sixty one who relies on R' Yonah's words has not lost, and as also written in Ma'adanei Melech chapter Keitzad Mevarchin page 53b to uphold R' Yonah's words — see there, and so is implied in Knesset HaGedolah itself; and even though in Darkei Moshe it implies one should forbid the cooked dish b'dieved if there are not sixty against the honey, and Magen Avraham agreed with him in note 11 — it appears because he left R' Yonah's words in uncertainty, and see in his words siman 456 note 3, and the main difficulty is from that which we say in Bechoros page 16 that milk of an animal is a novelty, since blood congealed and became milk it should be forbidden — we learn we follow the initial state, unlike R' Yonah's opinion, etc. — and if because of this it does not apply, and from honey there is no difficulty, for there it is different with milk, for one could say there should be no permission for any milk since all milk was formed from the prohibition of blood in the initial prohibition, even though it changed in appearance it did not change to a permitted matter — therefore they say it is a novelty that the Torah innovated. Unlike honey where the honey itself is permitted — if so, if they placed a forbidden thing in it that returns and transforms to make honey, we follow the present when it became a permitted thing which is honey — and why from Torah should we forbid it like other honey, since its taste and appearance are equal and it changed to a permitted thing — and it is clear. It is found there is no contradiction at all from that case of milk to R' Yonah's words regarding honey; also regarding musk (meaning what they call in foreign language pezum) that he wrote there R' Yonah — one can strain and distinguish similarly, but from there in any case there is room for doubt as Magen Avraham himself in siman 456, and from honey he left in uncertainty there is room for doubt, and as written — examine:",
    "8:ב": "Only that which they bring in barrels. The implication of the Rav's words is that even to eat it thus in its own form it is permitted in such a case, and so the Taz compelled from the Rav's language, unlike Magen Avraham who wrote the Rav's opinion is like some Acharonim that even honey brought in barrels is also forbidden to eat thus in its own form, only to make a drink from it before Pesach they permitted — and this does not fit well; and for practical halachah the Rav's relative concludes in his book that this law changes according to places and countries, and so I heard that honey of the land of Piham which is good and select and has no concern for forgery — but what is brought from other places such as the land of the Ger and Bruna or from Poland they are accustomed to forbid it except that which is brought in barrels while still with the comb on it — each place has its custom and its practice, and this is also the Rav's intent in what he wrote and the custom in these countries, and in Knesset HaGedolah he expanded greatly and was stringent regarding honey because he investigated in countries that they mix much flour and water into it until there are not sixty against the flour they mix into it — see note 16; and he also wrote there it became clear to him with certainty that when they extract honey from the comb it is impossible that they do not put a little water into it, and therefore one may not knead with it at all, for it is fruit juice with water — end of his words, and all this follows his view and other Acharonim as above siman 462; however according to what is written there that with a little water there is no concern for a house of concern if he performed guarding — yet we are not at all accustomed to rich matzah as explained several times:",
    "8:ג": "And dried grapes. Magen Avraham wrote in the name of Maharil: if he wishes to retain the opening of the sambur he can retain with our grapes — end of his words:",
    "8:ד": "Any dried fruits. But they are accustomed to make from them a drink — meaning they pour cold water on them and soak them until a drink is made, and so Magen Avraham testified that such is the custom in the community of Kalish; but one may not cook with them, and see there where he expanded on the reason for the custom:",
    "8:ה": "In a manner where there is no concern for chametz. And we do not decree this because of that, and similarly with honey in Bechoros it is permitted and we do not decree, because these things are common to bring honey from the hive and similarly fruits that an Israelite dries in his house he is careful about chametzing — unlike saffron which is not common to grow in his garden, therefore it is forbidden even what grew in his garden, as Levush wrote:",
    "8:ו": "(6) Even drinking is not allowed. And K. My brother-in-law, the rabbi in the SaZ, and I saw that he came to ask a question before the elders of the late Resh after Pesach, who was staying in the HOT Zucker and ordered his acquaintance to the Akoum Akal. And the 16th and the MA wrote a prohibition in the OT, Tzoker, or what we recite in the OT, Tzoker is permitted to stay in his house, MM used to sell it at first and later it is permitted, and this is the intention of the Rabbi in HaHa Tzoker Kandil, and not what we now recite Tzoker Kandil, etc., types of Tzoker at night, which hides various fruits and perfumes in which there is a complete pickle in it, per the content of their words. And really, at the beginning, one should be careful about the delay in these things, if he passed and delayed them, or mixes them up, and such a lot of loss or preventing the joy of Yom Tov, it is possible to forbid Dahari in siman 15 itself, which means only a prohibition to start it, and he wrote, and the Zeal, and the like. 20 and Zal and Zuker are accustomed to be careful if it is not a great need for medicine because peddlers mix in it several species of Akal and the above-mentioned taste is sufficient and I am concerned about good things. Therefore we will not interfere with it and Ethal we will interfere with it. We will not interfere with it before Pesach and Ethal will not be abolished. Before the teacher shoots, a smart investigation is needed as to how and what we will do, because this thing changes per the place and per the time, and to begin with, they tend to get worse in every matter, but what we are doing now, many of the people of the country are from Kilin, and they continue this mistake from the words of the rabbi, and the latter have already agreed It's their fault. And A. in siman 19 who wrote a Zucker model that is thin, very thin, there is a concern about pickling and also the drink made from Zucker that Korin Shiofi or Latvrig the Ashkenazis tend to make worse and A. L. Ras Tammu S. K. A and B: and A. M. Haril DIL an act that was like that, but it cannot be proved that there was a difference between the last and the first 19:",
    "8:ז": "That they call saffron. And see Yoreh De'ah note 114 from the law of saffron all year, and Magen Avraham wrote and Turei Zahav: the way is to soak it in beer and one must seal it in a room or make a partition before it, and if so the same applies to saffron — end quote, and see above end of siman 461 from the law of vessels dyed with saffron that one may not use them on Pesach:",
    "8:ח": "However we do not forbid their mixtures. It implies even in less than sixty it is also permitted, and see in note 16 from the law of honey, and in tsuker too one should not forbid mixtures even in types of night tsuker b'eyna, and in such a case one may be lenient as written in note 28 — see there:",
    "9:א": "And if they did not actually split open. Even if it softened until close to splitting it is also permitted, and see above note 7 regarding the matter in its reason — see there:",
    "9:ב": "(2) The stew is allowed. And the writing of the MA and ZL and the writing of the B. In the name of everything in which dam were found 3. Barleys in a strong stew, it is good, and lest they split, and the author omitted it in the 19th, because the author of siman 19 wrote the 16th in siman 19, section 9, in the name of the Mahrashal. Therefore, the author omitted the most beautiful KIL, because in this, the Rabbi and the Rash and Mordechai wrote a chapter every hour in the name of the Bahj and the association in the name of many geniuses. But what is supported by the words of the 16th in the name of the Rashel, here really the words of the Rashel in this are not necessary at all, and we do not find a difference in this in any ruling Dastama Amarinan Talta Zimna Hoi Hezka Da'a'g that it is a religious thing from the Alma MM is held this dish that has fallen into it wheat or barley' and there is some evidence for it Maha Damarinan Three is a cemetery neighborhood even though it is a religious thing from the Alma and A in Pet In Nazir this issue and in the Parshai of the S.H. and the De'at HaPoski' that I wrote to make it easier here Y'l Dachion that is found a teri or telta and not be split even if there is more also we will not be split and they did not make anything worse in the prohibition d'rabbanan Koli Hai All of this above is simply the opinion of the Puski' and the author and in the opinion of the Rabbi Bahga there is no Nafkota in all of this:",
    "9:ג": "Rather they forbid everything in any amount. Since the Rav did not write this gloss after what the Mechaber wrote, and those wheats or barleys they burn — he should have written burn everything as written nearby seif 10 in the gloss; rather it certainly appears the Rav's opinion where they did not actually split open is the opinion of all the great poskim to permit the cooked dish even in eating — if so, this stringency suffices to forbid in eating and not in benefit, and see beginning siman 467, and even in actually split open the opinion of many poskim is to sell to a gentile as the Mechaber's opinion in seif 10 — therefore in any case one should not be so stringent when it is not split open. And further the main reason for the custom to be stringent is per what Mordechai wrote in chapter Kol Sha'ah, and he is stringent because of she'ilamuta, and it was already explained above siman 467 that we hold chametz is not called she'ilamuta; also what Mordechai further wrote there and this is its language: and so in Semag one should be stringent since we are not expert in what is called fit to split open, and because of custom it does not forbid in uncertainty, and one who is lenient for Yom Tov joy has not lost — end of his words. It appears his intent is to rule like Mar Ukva that close to splitting is also forbidden in a cooked dish — but in truth the Rif and Rambam and BeHag and Rosh and Tur and Ra'avad and Agudah and Rokeach and Kol Bo all permit the cooked dish as long as they did not actually split open — in this one need not be expert, for all are expert in this to see if they actually split open or not; therefore it seems fit to rely on them and permit at least in benefit, and similarly Taz wrote in this end; however in Taz note 15 it implies forbidden in benefit, and in my humble opinion in a place of great loss and the like one may permit at least in benefit, and especially since Mordechai himself concluded one who is lenient for Yom Tov joy has not lost — it implies even in eating one may permit; but Bach wrote that nowadays even for Yom Tov joy they are not accustomed to be lenient, and so wrote Rabbeinu Yerucham; nevertheless for permission of benefit it appears to me as written, and see siman 467 notes 1 and 2. And Bach wrote that in any case we require that the wheat kernel softened, for if not there is no prohibition at all:",
    "9:ד": "Everything in any amount. And also the vessels used with them and the cooked dishes used with those vessels they also forbid, even though there are two any-amounts, as Magen Avraham wrote, unlike Taz as written above siman 467 note 5; also what Magen Avraham further challenged on Nehar Shalom I already wrote thus in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 39, and I brought his words briefly in siman 466 — see there:",
    "10:א": "Permitted to sell it to a gentile. So Bach wrote in the name of Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah who wrote thus in the name of Mahari of Paris, and so in Maharil in the name of the Rosh; however Mordechai himself challenged this and forbids, and so in Agudah, and like the Rav's ruling in the gloss, and see above siman 467 notes 2 and 3 — all this specifically with a split wheat kernel as written in note 26:",
    "10:ב": "And if it was found on the eighth day. It implies even if the mixture occurred during Pesach (and even if he transgressed and retained intentionally as explained above siman 467) they are lenient in a mixture of any amount on the last day which is rabbinic to retain l'chatchila, and specifically if found on the eighth day, but if found during Pesach one must burn and destroy everything on chol ha-mo'ed, and if found on the seventh of Pesach see above beginning siman 466 how one should conduct — see there the implication of the Acharonim as written here:",
    "10:ג": "Permitted to retain it. Meaning in a mixture of any amount remove and it is permitted; but in something that is complete chametz forbidden d'oraisa, even on the last Yom Tov it is forbidden in any case rabbinically — so agreed the Acharonim, and it is plain:",
    "11:א": "And so they are accustomed during Pesach. Levush expanded to challenge the Rav on this, and his opinion is to permit in dough in every matter that is not taste imparted in cold, and Magen Avraham wrote in note 19 and this is its language: and I found written that the Gaon Maharal of Prague ruled to permit, but Maharash of Austria wrote to Rosh of Lublin to forbid, and so is the custom of most of the world, and in Krakow it appears to me it depends on custom — end quote. But in truth even though Levush reconciles the Tur's words not to forbid in cold, behold Mordechai forbids in meat salting even if a wheat kernel was found after salting — see there; and it is possible he forbade because there were cracks as written siman 465, and so Bach wrote here in the name of the Geonim; therefore he wrote regarding meat that has cracks it is forbidden by law in cold, and similarly in uncertainty whether it was salted from above it is also forbidden by law — end quote Magen Avraham. And still the obligation of explanation is not discharged, for since he forbids in meat because there are cracks, from this we say the wheat kernel imparts taste in any amount in cold and enters the cracks in the meat — if so all the more one should forbid in cold water or in dough more than cracks in meat; and this distinction is not applicable except regarding another solid lump like meat that has no cracks and is not a soft penetrating thing. And Taz concludes that even though for other cold one may permit like Levush, nevertheless in dough through handling in dough the wheat kernel is crushed and chametzs and forbids the entire dough during Pesach if it softened (and similarly in chametz bread one should be stringent even in cold lest one be concerned for crumbs), and so the Rav's relative raised in his book — thus appears the conclusion of Magen Avraham and Bach and Maharshal. Nevertheless it appears to me according to what is written above note 18 that if the wheat kernel is not soaked one may permit in benefit b'eyna — if so all the more here in cold, for the opinion of many poskim is to permit in every matter; and it is possible the Rav's words and so they are accustomed also refer only to a soaked wheat kernel, as the Mechaber mentioned — if not he should have glossed here too that even if it is not split like in seif 9; rather it must be that since it is a cold thing his intent is not to be stringent at all if it is not soaked, since there is another side to be lenient; however Bach and other Acharonim who wrote one forbids only if the wheat kernel softened — from this they hold it is forbidden here even if it did not actually split open; and it is possible the Rav's gloss is also thus, and he did not gloss here because he relies on what he glossed in seif 9 — the same for all of them; nevertheless this is only to forbid in eating and not in benefit as written above note 26, and one who is lenient even in eating has not lost, for it is possible even if split one may be lenient in cold b'eyna, as the Rav wrote in the gloss and so they are accustomed — meaning one may be lenient b'eyna or in a time of distress, and so appears to me the essence:",
    "11:ב": "(2) Here is found and here is heath. And the MA Dam wrote that the matzah is full of water and moistened from the other side. If it appears that it was in the water and in the pulp, all the pulp should be prohibited: And MA, if it was a hangman that was swallowed from a place other than water and fell into the matzah, everything is permitted per the case, because you will be fulfilled in the order either to make it easier or to make it worse, and there are no cases of doubt. Or from piece to piece, but from time to time in Hada, a piece is forbidden (and so on the Farah BID C. MA, Section 18) such as if he has a piece of pulp in his hand and a cut from it, and a piece of pulp remains in his hand, and a piece of pulp is found on it, they are forbidden, if it was before (nor should it be said that there is a shadow of doubt, it is sufficient that the Dashma was not soured because there is a shadow of doubt, lack of knowledge, as well as the consent of the 16th in Section 14) And refer to the answer of MB C. Set, what also divides in the matter of law here is found and as it was, and in the answer of CHC C. MG. Katha Batsho' of the Haram of Lublin C. A. A case of one who took cabbage from the barrels in a bowl and they were taken to another place and there a hata was found on it and the cabbage that was in his bag was left. But if he is found later in the barrels, even the cabbage in the bowl is not allowed to be marinated, lest the hetta was previously in the Akal barrels:",
    "12:א": "(1) If Melgo the hen. In Rokhah C. Rza and it is also in Mordechai P. 27 and Zal an incident where a rooster caught a rooster with its feather in a bowl of sour cream and it is a second vessel and it was forbidden to eat it as a damarinen blood part of a city sinning into it boils an akal therefore 20 16 here Dafi' in 27 is forbidden and this is per the opinion of the rabbi above C. 28 In regard to the matter of the two vessels of the dish being strict on Passover, it seems to the Rabbis that there is a dispute here in the rule of Damda, who brought evidence that a deiri is boiling into it, meaning of Damdin, the infusion of Myri, and of Mimri Mikri, the second vessel, called the bowl, and KMS, 3C above, C. TNA, Sec. Dahametz, after all, they learned from the A. page of Dam Hatat, and the pharmacist C. Tela ruled on the matter of other prohibitions to allow a second vessel, but surely Demiry Medin, transfusion, and M.M. for the law should be made stricter when the Rama ruled above, the sign of Tamaz Dish to make it worse, if the hand is disgusted with it with chametz, some of the judges decided to forbid Effi. In the second vessel also the words of the apothecary and Mordechai must be reconciled with Damiri in the second vessel, therefore it should not be made easy as above:",
    "12:ב": "Soaked wheat kernel. It is possible since the Rav did not gloss here as below seif 9 to forbid even if not soaked, because here since there is another uncertainty that perhaps afterward it fell we are not so stringent; however Taz wrote the Rav follows his view to forbid even if not soaked, only whatever softened — permitted to sell — end quote, and see above notes 18 and 22 — see there:",
    "12:ג": "The chicken is forbidden. Agreement of Rishonim and Acharonim that we do not say regarding a chicken here it was found here it was, and now it fell; and Magen Avraham wrote to distinguish that we do not say it was born now where there is no presumption of permission previously as stated in chapter 1 of Chullin and Niddah, and so Taz — on the contrary all impurities as at the time they are found (and see responsum Mishna Berurah and Tzemach Tzedek that I hinted above note 32); and even for Beit Shammai in chapter 1 of Niddah one may be stringent here since there are two adverse factors — wheats in the body and wheats that fell from outside, as the Talmud answers there page 3a regarding a rodent found in an alley — see there, and see Rambam chapter 15 and chapter 18 of Laws of Impurity, and see in my book Torat HaShelamim:",
    "12:ד": "And there is one who forbids. See above note 31:",
    "12:ה": "For we say perhaps afterward it fell there. Bach wrote and Rivash wrote in the name of Rashba: if a wheat kernel was found in a vessel in which they rinsed the chicken, we say here it was found here it was as with blemishes, and we do not say it came from the chicken — and so he ruled in practical halachah — end quote; Magen Avraham challenged this from Rashba's own words that he ruled if a wheat kernel was found in matzah they forbid the dough as written in note 11, and we do not say it fell now, and he expanded further to challenge this — many difficulties, and concludes it deals when they salted the chicken and afterward rinsed in a cold vessel, and afterward a wheat kernel was found in the vessel, and there is concern it was in the chicken and was salted with it — therefore we are not concerned for this since the wheat kernel was found in the vessel we do not say it was in the chicken because we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place; but in matzah which is from time to time as written — there is no dispute at all, unlike Rama's opinion — such is the content of his words there; even though his words at first glance are correct and he follows his view that from time to time aliba di-chol everyone establishes presumptive prohibition as he wrote in note 32; however in what he concluded that in matzah it is from time to time as written, and his intent to explain thus Rashba's opinion — this is astonishing, for on the contrary in matzah it is from place to place if not that it deals that dough remained in his hand as written in note 32 in his name, and this is against Rashba's language and implication, and the Rav's relative wrote in the name of Darkei Moshe many supports for this view — see there:",
    "12:ו": "From the force of double uncertainty. See above note 10:",
    "12:ז": "Or in a whole wheat kernel. Tur wrote here, and the Rosh agreed that we do not derive prohibition from impurity, and it is like digested; and in Yoreh De'ah end of siman 83 he wrote: pure that swallowed an impure fish is forbidden, for it is not considered digested since it is whole — from this Tur holds that when it is not whole he holds it is like digested; and that which he wrote here we do not derive prohibition from impurity that it is like digested is specifically when it is whole before us, unlike when it is broken we see it was digested before us — for presumably when the chicken ate it the wheat was whole, as Agudah chapter 1 of Bechoros, and so Shach explained Yoreh De'ah there the Tur's words (unlike some Acharonim there who explained the Tur's words per R' Meir of Pontise) — and this explanation is correct and essential, and so is clear from Tosafos' rulings chapter 1 of Bechoros, even though they seized in plainness that we do not learn prohibition from impurity for digestion — nevertheless Tosafos wrote there briefly that one forbids only when a whole wheat kernel is found, and it is found he ruled well here to be lenient through digestion when the wheat kernel is not whole — see there in Peri Chadash:",
    "12:ח": "And chickens were salted there. Magen Avraham wrote: not specifically chickens but the same applies to meat, for here it deals with a whole wheat kernel where there is no digestion — end quote. And in Olat Shabbat he wrote: for amplification he cited chickens even though wheat kernels are commonly found in them — nevertheless no uncertainty emerges and we say perhaps afterward it fell; there is also another uncertainty that perhaps he holds like those who say it is not taste imparted at all in cold, which is like double uncertainty; Bach is stringent in these laws, and challenged the Rav — and the essence is like the Rav's words and so agreed the Rav's relative in his book and Taz in particular if the wheat kernel is not split open — plainly one should not be stringent as written above note 31, and without softening the wheat kernel one may be lenient in every matter, and so agreed Bach and the Acharonim:",
    "12:ט": "In uncertainties of rabbinic prohibition. See Bach what he wrote on this, and see what is written nearby note 40; and Nekudot HaKesef wrote chapter Orlah and he is Shach in Yoreh De'ah siman 111 regarding two pots one of permission and one of prohibition, and before him two pieces one of permission and one of prohibition and this fell into that — if the prohibition is rabbinic it is permitted, for we say I say permission fell into permission, even if the permission in the pot is not majority — on this Nekudot HaKesef wrote: it is possible that for stringent rabbinic any-amount prohibitions we do not say I say, even in rabbinic prohibitions in less than majority — end quote; and Peri Chadash there challenged at the beginning of siman 111 without proof and wrote: since rabbinic any-amount prohibition we follow leniency in every uncertainty like all rabbinic uncertainty, and his words are not compelled to be lenient against Nekudot HaKesef's opinion, especially since we find many stringencies they were stringent on Pesach even in mixtures of any amount even in uncertainty; and Magen Avraham wrote: one who took into his mouth a piece of matzah and a piece of chicken and ate both together — one may permit the chicken or meat, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place, and we say it fell now in cold; and similarly the matzah one may permit; but if the meat rests in a bowl and he ate the meat from it, it is forbidden, for from time to time we establish presumptive prohibition, for perhaps it was in the bowl when he poured the meat into it (nevertheless according to majority it is recognizable whether the wheat kernel was cooked or baked with it or not) — however if they shook the bowl from place to place when it cooled we attribute to leniency, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place — end quote:",
    "12:י": "That was rinsed in water. Magen Avraham wrote: explanation — even though the wheat kernel was found inside while the meat was still soaking, nevertheless it is a rabbinic uncertainty, for there are those who permit in cold, and his words are not compelled to explain the Rav's opinion — see note 38; or scalded — meaning and afterward the wheat kernel was found, for one can say it fell now. And that which Rama ruled above seif 2 that double uncertainty does not help — that is for eating the wheats themselves, but the mixtures are permitted. Bach wrote in one uncertainty even to retain it is forbidden, even b'eyna, and in the end permitted to retain, and all the more permitted to sell them to a gentile and not for eating, for that uncertainty that perhaps it was digested is not a full uncertainty, for one can say digestion does not help in prohibition as written; and specifically complete chametz such as bread or dough, but a wheat kernel found in water permits the cooked dish cooked in it beforehand, for it is double uncertainty that perhaps it fell now, and if you say it fell before, perhaps it did not soften until now, and without softening there is no prohibition at all even according to our custom — end quote Magen Avraham:",
    "13:א": "Day-old. But if it is not day-old, even the hag'elah water itself is permitted on Pesach, since they hag'eled before Pesach and they are not day-old as explained above siman 461, and even fat that one melted before Pesach in a chametz vessel that is not day-old is permitted on Pesach as ruled in siman 467 seif 5 in the gloss, even though the Rav decided above that remove is forbidden on Pesach — all this is plain, and Bach toiled in vain on this — see there:",
    "13:ב": "During Pesach. Bach wrote that even if they became mixed before Pesach in a matter nullified in sixty (even though it is also taste imparted by taste imparted of permission) nevertheless one may not use that water l'chatchila since one can easily find other water easily, and it is not for a cooked dish which is b'dieved, and if we forbid eating it on Pesach — end quote; Magen Avraham did not cite his language accurately; Taz wrote that sometimes even b'dieved it is forbidden in such a case if the sour waters remained above and did not mix, for they settled and stood and are as if in their own form unless he drew from it beforehand several times that through this it mixes — end quote. In my humble opinion one should not forbid b'dieved even though it was not nullified, nevertheless since it occurred before Pesach it is taste imparted by taste imparted of permission, and one should not forbid b'dieved — see siman 467 in the gloss and what is written there and siman 461 and 462 — see there:",
    "13:ג": "Because they became mixed. They become one lump — if so even for those who permit in cold here it is forbidden since the body of chametzing became mixed; and if a wheat kernel or other chametz is found in this pit, it depends on the dispute the Mechaber wrote above seif 12 whether he forbids in cold during Pesach — see above note 31; and see Taz, Beit Yosef, Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah, Rokeach siman 286, and Shulchan Lechem siman 63 who forbid if chametz is found in a well — and it appears it deals in a matter where there is concern for crumbs as written above; and so in responsum Rashba siman 176 — see there; and Bach wrote in this end in the name of Maharil that one should be careful for one who used on Pesach wells of gentiles or other wells where they are not careful all year that one should strain the water in a clean garment each time he draws — end quote; and see above siman 467 from the law of straining regarding vinegar and wine, and above siman 455 from the law of drawing water:",
    "14:א": "Rinsing suffices. In general in cold without gravy, all agree it does not forbid — end quote Magen Avraham; even though in a chicken it is impossible without a little moisture, unlike in cold it does not absorb according to everyone except through a little salting or through handling in dough and such; Responsum Tzemach Tzedek: question — if a little beer remained in a vessel and he drew water into it and rinsed other meat after salting on Pesach — it is close to absorption and has the law of a wheat kernel salted with the chicken for each opinion in this seif. I already challenged him in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 6 note 23, for water nullifies the power of salt and it has only the law of cold and is not forbidden through rinsing alone, even if there are cracks, for the Rav ruled above siman 465 to forbid in goose — specifically there where he rubs well in its crop or scalds inside as written above note 31 in the name of Mordechai, and see above siman 465 note 6 — unlike mere rinsing (and with this one can also explain what Bach challenged here on the Rosh — see there); and mixture of any amount — nevertheless because of Pesach stringency I raised there one should peel a little of the meat he rinsed, and it is a stringency in itself:",
    "14:ב": "After salting. Magen Avraham wrote: explanation — while still in its salt; but if found after it was salted and rinsed, we say it fell now, for if you say it was on it beforehand it would have fallen during rinsing when he rinsed, and specifically when found on the chicken, but if found inside the chicken it was certainly previously — see Niddah page 3 regarding a box that has rims — end quote; and see siman 467 note 6 where I wrote pure salted and impure bland is also permitted here, and it deals when the wheat kernel was also salted with the chicken — see there; and I saw meticulous people are careful on Pesach before scalding and salting to open the chicken well or cut it in two and check well that no wheat kernel is found inside, and Knesset HaGedolah wrote one who does so is zealous and rewarded — end quote:",
    "14:ג": "And to permit the rest through peeling. And if he transgressed and cooked without peeling, see siman 467 note 9; and in Magen Avraham he wrote here various distinctions in this law, and the essence as written there; and regarding the essential law many Acharonim in their responsa, for some ruled to burn everything and some Acharonim permit the rest of the pieces entirely, and so the Rav himself ruled in responsum siman 108; and the essence appears to me like Taz's conclusion and this is its language: and in my humble opinion for practical conduct one should act thus: if a wheat kernel is found on salted meat dry without gravy, one may permit retaining everything, only where it was found one peels, and the rest may be retained until after Pesach; but if the wheat kernel is found in the gravy, then it is considered like cooked, for in a slight measure it is like cooked in gravy — then everything resting in the gravy should be forbidden, but what rests above the gravy one may retain until after Pesach or do as Rama wrote at the end to sell to a gentile what is not in the gravy — end quote; and his words appear correct, and all the more if the wheat kernel is not split open one should not be stringent in salting at all as written above note 26; and it is possible the Rav's gloss too is only stringent with a split wheat kernel that the Mechaber mentioned, even though Taz follows his view that it deals even if not split open — see notes 26 and 31, and see responsum Maharam of Lublin siman 91 where his opinion also appears to be lenient regarding salting even during Pesach — see there:",
    "15:א": "Split wheat kernel. Knesset HaGedolah wrote: since the Rav did not gloss here too even if not split open, it appears he holds if not split open one forbids only in roasting as much as removal — end quote; however the opinion of other Acharonim is not so — see above note 26 and Machatzit HaShekel:",
    "15:ב": "Place of bubbling. It deals with a thin chicken, for if not it bubbles and the whole thing is forbidden as explained above siman 467 — see there:",
    "15:ג": "According to how they turn the spit. See in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 28 note 2 what I challenged on the separation, and I wrote specifically for rabbinic any-amount prohibition we are concerned for this, unlike other prohibitions — see there; and I saw Magen Avraham also aligned with this distinction literally as written there:",
    "15:ד": "When they turn the spit. The Rav's relative wrote in his book: if the spit is not turned, it appears from Rama's opinion that the law of roasting is like the law of salting — end quote:",
    "15:ה": "Touching one another. Magen Avraham wrote: if the chicken is fatty, even if they do not touch, it forbids everything — end quote; nevertheless when not split open it appears to me one should not be stringent as written above:",
    "16:_": "The bird is permitted. And even peeling is not needed, for the removal is in the place of peeling; Bach ruled to forbid the whole thing, and the Rav's relative decides to forbid in eating and permit in benefit, and other Acharonim agree to permit entirely in scalding:"
  },
  "output/siman_467/beur-hagra/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א": "Seif 1 — just as, etc. Since Rava needed to sell it:",
    "1:ב": "Rather he sells it. The Rambam's version: keva keva so that, etc.; and there is an alternate version \"to an Israelite,\" and it refers to what was said initially to sell to Eretz Yisrael; but to an Israelite, since he informs him, it is permitted in any amount, as written in chapter 3 of Demai mishnah 3 R' Yehuda certainly permits, etc.:",
    "2:א": "Seif 2 — this grain. Therefore Rava did not ask whether they split open. Hagahot Maimoni and Ra'avan there:",
    "2:ב": "From the force of a double uncertainty. As written in Kesubos 9a:",
    "2:ג": "But not to eat it. Since he has permissible grain, a double uncertainty does not help, as written at the beginning of Yoma 3b; and its uncertainty is forbidden, and if it became mixed, etc. Mordechai end of chapter 3 of Avodah Zarah, and see Yoreh De'ah siman 110.",
    "3:_": "Seif 3 — wheat, etc., presumptive prohibition, etc. As written in chapter 3 of Gittin: one who lends money, etc., even though death is common, as written, and in Sukkah 24a:",
    "4:_": "Sed Shana 20. Dahzoki and Holkin after the majority and Kms in Gitin there is the bringer of a divorce and they put 20 and stated in the Gm that most are sick for life:",
    "5:_": "Seif 5 — grain, etc. As written in chapter Kol Sha'ah that it chametzs while attached, and therefore in such a case:",
    "6:_": "So Hatim, 20. Dahzouki, 20, as above, and Kms Begitin, where he assumes fruits, 20, if they are lost, 20, and concludes stated in the Gm, Mala'a, of an examination, and Arshi, 45, Mala'a, 20:",
    "7:_": "SZ Yisrael 20. Faded like chametz on which an avalanche fell in P. 27 and Rashi's KMS in PA Gavi who makes his house a treasure and another DSS Lest we miss out and ETL let us miss out lest we become stale and therefore it is permissible to linger in the SAB. Rashba:",
    "8:א": "Seif 8 — honey, etc., presumptive prohibition, etc., as above; and further that even if flour became mixed in it there is no prohibition, as the Rosh wrote on the Yerushalmi mentioned above R' Chananel, etc., it would be forged honey with flour, etc., and the concern is distant that perhaps water became mixed in the honey:",
    "8:ב": "(2) Who is stricter? They are accustomed to mixing semolina with their tea, and for this he said in Yerushalmi that it is necessary to burn it because of the honey, it is not in the Rash and Kmash Tos in P. Ksh. and in offerings on red vinegar because the barley in the water will become sour, and they are forbidden in the 23rd because they get drunk by forgery Kmash Baa 44 B. Hamra 20 and 20 in Thos. But if he didn't see it attractive, etc.",
    "8:ג": "And figs, etc., hanging, etc. — that figs are accustomed to mix flour into them for sweetness; and grapes are accustomed to dry them in an oven near bread or after they removed the bread from the oven; and in places where they do not mix flour and dry in the sun it is permitted — see in Tosafos Avodah Zarah there s.v. hai chala, etc., therefore, etc., since there are places, etc., and so is implied in the Gemara hai chala, etc., that they mix — meaning they are accustomed to mix — it implies only in places where they are accustomed:",
    "8:ד": "And therefore they were accustomed. Like grapes as above:",
    "8:ה": "And tsuker. That they mix flour into it:",
    "8:ו": "But on Yom Tov. However on other days, even those who do not mix, it is forbidden because of confusion:",
    "8:ז": "And the custom. That they forge with flour to establish its appearance, and so they place on se'or to restore its appearance:",
    "8:ח": "negeliach. For they soak it in barley water:",
    "8:ט": "However. For regarding rabbinic any-amount we do not establish presumptive prohibition b'dieved:",
    "9:א": "Seif 9 — cooked dish. In chapter Kol Sha'ah 40a — taught: one may not soak, etc., R' Chananel: they did not split open, etc., and Shmuel said, etc., and we conclude in chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah regarding Torah law we follow the stringent and regarding scribal law we follow the lenient — and so too here since mixtures of any amount is rabbinic:",
    "9:ב": "Or wheat. To exclude from Ra'em who forbids wheat per his words even if they did not split open at all since they have splitting, and so wrote Eliyah Rabbah there s.v. ve'yeish omrim, etc., and he brings proof from the latter chapter of Avodah Zarah 65b — but it is not so, for the conclusion there of Kol Sha'ah is that wheat is lighter than barley, and that case in Avodah Zarah is not relevant here; and further he brings proof from that cellar steeped in chaff and they did not require there split open, and the Ran wrote that is not proof since there they were split open — and see below seif 2; and also one should not read that wheat even if split open is permitted, for if so the guarding below is unnecessary. But in Tosefta chapter 3 it implies a distinction between wheat and barley, for it teaches there: one soaks wheat and barley in water and they chametzed — forbidden; R' Yose says: one who soaks barley in water and they split open — forbidden, and in vinegar, etc., and it teaches it in the name of R' Yose from the latter part — and in vinegar, etc., for he stated it as written in the Gemara:",
    "9:ג": "The cooked dish is permitted. For in rabbinic law we follow leniency, as written in chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah, even though its essence is from Torah law as written in chapter 5 that we say it is different since it does not exist as in normal circumstances — even though its essence is from Torah — Ran:",
    "9:ד": "And the custom is not, etc. That they conduct like Mar Ukva and we are not expert in the measure fit for splitting. Semag and Mordechai. And it appears to me the reason for the custom as written in seif 2 this grain, etc., and the Rif holds that even in such a case we require split open, and in the incident of the cellar as the Ran wrote that there they were split open as above, and so in Tosefta one soaks barley, etc., as above:",
    "10:א": "Seif 10 — chicken, etc. As written in Avodah Zarah 65b — that crate of wheat, etc., and Mordechai chapter 2 and so too Ramban there from Yerushalmi chapter 3 of Orlah on mishnah: a garment dyed with orlah peels — it must burn; and we learned: if one removed from it a saddlecloth and wove the garment — forbidden in benefit; and we learned: if yayin nesech fell into a pit — all is forbidden in benefit; and we learned Rabbenu Shimon says: sell all of it to a gentile except the value of the yayin nesech in it — R' Yirmiyah bar Acha questioned: who is like that? There gentiles are not accustomed to buy wine from gentiles, but here gentiles are accustomed to buy garments from an Israelite — therefore anything that is not customary to buy from a gentile is permitted in all prohibitions, and so is the opinion of the Rosh in Orlah there, and Tosafos Zevachim 72b s.v. kulam and Yevamos chapter 1 s.v. kulam, etc., except that Mordechai challenged that the drop around the wheat kernel is chametz and it is like wine in wine that is permitted only for yayin nesech of rabbis and not d'oraisa as written above; but in Milchamot there he wrote that other prohibitions even if their prohibition is d'oraisa nevertheless for stam yeinam we compare them, for we are more stringent with stam yeinam than all prohibitions since it is in any amount, and we say in chapter 2 of Pesachim and chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah, etc., that this d'oraisa, etc., whatever the rabbis enacted, etc., and specifically complete yayin nesech which is among prohibitions atop prohibitions we are stringent, etc., and so wrote the Ran in Avodah Zarah there — except that he wrote regarding redemption that even in stam yeinam specifically barrel in barrel and not wine in wine — see what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 142:",
    "10:ב": "That it is not sold, etc. — meaning one need not say except the chametz, etc., and as in that cellar of wheat in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah that Rava permitted to sell them, etc., anonymously — Mordechai there siman 491 in the name of R' Yechiel of Paris — see there, and Tosafos Chullin 99 s.v. vehalachta, etc.:",
    "10:ג": "And in these countries, etc. — for they hold specifically in stam yeinam the rabbis were lenient, as written above in Yoreh De'ah letter 1 practical halachah, etc., and see Mordechai double uncertainty 491 and siman 493, and even though in other matters we do not distinguish between stam yeinam and chametz as written there 34a and chapter Kol Sha'ah, etc., whatever the rabbis enacted, etc., here the Gemara distinguishes explicitly:",
    "10:ד": "And if it was found on the eighth day, etc. — Magen Avraham, and the same in seif 9 in a cooked dish wheat and barley were found:",
    "11:א": "(1) C.A. If it is found in the same way. A dam is not in a state of desolation. It is permissible for a damastama. It was not in water. They are damshum. And if it was in flour or in a paste, it is not in a hurry to leave more than the sticky dough.",
    "11:ב": "One removes, etc. As written in chapter Keitzad Tzolin 97b: one takes its place — a drop from its gravy on the flour, one scoops, etc.:",
    "11:ג": "And there is one, etc. — Magen Avraham. And further that the water in the dough, etc., and according to what the Rosh wrote regarding a se'or house that kneading is like hot with gravy that forbids in all of it, as written in Chullin 96b and 108a:2, and all the more so in matzah that was baked:",
    "11:ד": "And they permit, etc., and we say. As written in Kesubos 75b:",
    "11:ה": "And if it was found — specifically from place to place we say here it was found, but not from time to time as written there while still in her father's house we do not say it was born now, and in Niddah 3:2 one who wrings out, etc., — he did not say except, etc., even though from place to place they argue — Chizkiyah and R' Yochanan, for R' Yehuda holds in impurity we are stringent — see Tosafos there 4a s.v. ve'mai. And one can say Chizkiyah holds that even in impurity we do not establish presumptive prohibition, and nevertheless Chizkiyah concedes to R' Mona as written there — and Magen Avraham note 18:",
    "11:ו": "(6) But first. It is nullified in S. and A.G. from the first point of the Rashba's opinion here and here it must be forbidden even before Passover, one should not feel it because it is a detsonan and A. 14 C. K. H. Set in the Haga, and if one does not know 2. and 6.",
    "12:א": "Seif 12 — if they scalded, etc. — for we do not say after they removed the chicken it fell, for we do not say from time to time as above in seif 11:",
    "12:ב": "And if an embedded wheat kernel was found, etc., there is nothing in this. So wrote the Tur: since the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste, and even complete chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason, and Beit Yosef wrote — even though from the words of the Rosh in chapter Kol Sha'ah in that case in seif 11 that a wheat kernel was found in dough he wrote one should not be concerned perhaps the wheat kernel was in water — forty days — for if it was in water it forbids in cold — there it deals during Pesach in any amount, and here it deals before Pesach; and proof from what the Tur wrote that in cold water the wheat kernel does not impart taste, etc., and if during Pesach why mention imparting taste — it forbids in any amount; and therefore he said there is nothing in this because the reason that it does not absorb in cold is because it is any amount — therefore some forbid during Pesach, and their reason is that they hold as Ribba in Tosafos Pesachim 76a s.v. tania that both sixty and cold rinsing are required — only its prohibition is slight unlike during Pesach; and it deals even in less than sixty, not like Magen Avraham as above:",
    "12:ג": "And there is one who permits. Meaning since any amount is rabbinic we are lenient in its uncertainty as written in Shabbos 34a and Eruvin 5b and other places, and as the Rif wrote in a cooked dish where a wheat kernel was found as above in seif 9 — even though Rama does not hold thus, he wrote this opinion here because in cold or b'eyna one relies on this opinion as written:",
    "12:ד": "(4) And there are others. R. S.S. Lest after he removed the chicken the hen fell and the atal that was in the hen lest it be digested in the hen Kmash PIA Dahlot gives for the matter of digestion how long it will stay in its intestines 20 and fowls and fish so that it falls to the light and burns up and they are forbidden from impure Kmash at the end of Aa as the way they said in the purity so In the PG Dafashim Sh.M. a dachal that is not impure to impure food 20, but the Toss in the offerings of the 7th 1st and the Harsh in the Ahloth there they made a difficulty from the Mash in the firstfruits 7b 2 a pure fish that swallowed an unclean fish is forbidden to eat and in the name of R. Hai Dish to divide between prohibition and impurity etc. Impurity to be cleansed yes' and more you will want hard fish in the offerings there that I am the meat of your ways etc. I am the bones of Dakushi and that is why they say that there are goats because of cattle etc. to be small for eating impure, impure food and even for offerings just because of the sacrifice please to your poor and you will also want it lest it be the firstfruits of Atia as the Rabbi who said in the flocks there in fowls and fish from above and without the presence of Meiri. And Rabban 20 Daff to the TAK lest the fish be swallowed a moment before by its side and it did not stay for a period of digestion, etc. in the holin 11 1 and Arshi there 45 Who is not business' 20 and there is no digestion but from life as a 20' in the addition there and the Rabbi brought there a dog that ate the flesh of the dead person 20 died if it did not stay in its stomach awake that it lived for 3 days above the house is unclean and in poultry 20 and 30 from the power of SS And per the Ravan:",
    "12:ה": "And regarding practical halachah. The conclusion of Tosafos there and Rosh there that whether for prohibition or impurity in anything whole that is recognizable that it was not digested and not chewed in his mouth, such as whole wheat in cattle dung and whole fish — therefore he cited fish that swallowed while whole, and see there and forty days from Tosafos in conclusion they are not concerned for these resolutions — therefore he ruled here in cold or b'eyna as the first version that we are lenient in uncertainty of any amount since it is rabbinic, and in a broken wheat kernel in a chicken he cited as Tosafos' conclusion that there is digestion only that he was concerned for Ra'avan's uncertainty and permits through double uncertainty; but in whole or in meat, since there is only one uncertainty and it is in hot and b'eyna is not stringent per Tosafos' version and their conclusion as above:",
    "13:א": "Seif 13 — day-old. Otherwise remove and it is permitted according to Shulchan Aruch in siman 467 seif 10. Beit Yosef:",
    "13:ב": "During Pesach. Before Pesach it is nullified in sixty in the pit. Beit Yosef. And even though hag'alah does not help during Pesach as in siman 452 seif 1 in the gloss, and even according to Shulchan Aruch who permits remove — nevertheless for day-old it does not help, and if so as Magen Avraham in the name of Darkei Moshe that he hag'eled before the time of prohibition and they descended into the pit during Pesach — even though it is taste imparted by taste imparted, nevertheless l'chatchila it is forbidden as Yoreh De'ah siman 95 seif 2 in the gloss, apart from the matters being strained in themselves — one for Shulchan Aruch there who permits even l'chatchila, and even for those who forbid there it is only because of custom, and here from the law it is forbidden; and further even for those who forbid there in such a case it is three taste imparted by taste imparted that is permitted as Tosafos end of Avodah Zarah and Rosh chapter Kol Sha'ah and as written above siman 432 seif 1 — except they hold like those who say that whatever is boiling releases, boiling absorbs, does not absorb — and according to this even during Pesach one may hag'el in any case only that they should not rest from their boiling, and as written there:",
    "13:ג": "Because. Magen Avraham:",
    "14:א": "Seif 14 — if it was found, etc. Magen Avraham and as written in Pesachim 76a: cold into hot, etc., and see there:",
    "14:ב": "And if after. Salting is like boiling, roasting is like boiling as in Chullin 97b, and peeling suffices as in Pesachim there — one peels its place, and even for the stringent in roasting — as much as removal; and he compared to a drop from gravy, etc., there in salting they agree peeling suffices — Rosh in Chullin there, and see Yoreh De'ah siman 91 and siman 105, and all the more so per his words they objected in Chullin 112a, but roasting requires peeling — boiling is discussed, not like Tosafos there 96b s.v. ad and Rashi Pesachim 76a that cold is discussed, and so in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 91 that in roasting boiling is meant, and if so even in roasting peeling suffices — see below siman 467 seif 1 and what is written:",
    "14:ג": "And there are those who are stringent, etc. Since its prohibition is in any amount and specifically around it one is concerned for that any amount. Kol Bo in the name of Ra'avad, and he holds the peel forbids again, not like Tosafos Chullin 96 s.v. afilu except that in other prohibitions since the prohibition absorbed in the peel does not forbid because it spreads in its depth as written here — and there are views against it unlike here — and one asks: let it make its depth nevelah and forbid again as Rabbenu Tam wrote there 101 in s.v. shekadam. And Or Zarua, etc.:",
    "14:ד": "And if they were salted, etc. — meaning and he does not know perhaps it touched all of them — Magen Avraham and as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 9 and if it is not known, etc.:",
    "14:ה": "And there are those who are stringent, etc. Magen Avraham and as in Chullin 97a — salting is different because it bubbles and salting is like boiling like roasting, and even though taste does not pass from piece to piece without gravy as in Chullin 108a:2 and Tosafos there s.v. tipach, etc., here there is concern perhaps it touched all of them and forbids all of them in any amount:",
    "14:ו": "(6) The main thing is to prohibit etc. Damdina is not forbidden even to those who require S. in salting, because they are not well versed in the matter that belongs to the oiliness of Masach in chametz, as in siman 19, stated in the Haga only for the sake of materiality, and therefore there is no stricter in the Sika to prohibit all of them, lest they touch only the rind, which is forbidden in Madina, and Ama:",
    "14:ז": "And if he wishes, etc. — for otherwise he permits in Shulchan Aruch above seif 11, and even for those who forbid there because its prohibition is d'oraisa, but for rabbinic prohibitions not, and all the more so here which is only a stringency in itself:",
    "15:א": "Seif 15 — chicken, etc., one cuts, etc. As in Chullin 96b and Pesachim 76b, and as Ra'avad wrote in siman 14 that one deepens, etc., as written there:",
    "15:ב": "And there are those who forbid, etc. — for in their days they did not turn the spit as in Chullin 111a, and Rashba there s.v. halachta, etc., and he holds turning the spit is like something that bubbles that forbids all of it as written 97a, but taste does not pass from piece to piece as written there 108 and Tosafos s.v. tipat:",
    "15:ג": "And so there is, etc. — for he holds turning the spit is like shaking and covering that spreads through all the pieces as written 108a:2 — see there in Tosafos:",
    "16:א": "Seif 16 — the bird is permitted. Taz and Magen Avraham:",
    "16:ב": "(2) And Hatta etc. Dela etc. You should learn the prohibition of impurity. column. Therefore, there is no division between peace or brokenness and Atos' Davkorot 7 2 and in Chulin 14:45 Raba said and took this the T'i' Datus' all the other excuses are narrow what divides between Salima and Shebra' and that is not what makes it more difficult there is a dilemma for the right, even though they pushed stated in the Tos' and also the Rabbin's so that falling into the light is a little thing and also in the supplement there he said in the fowls, etc. immediately Only T. Dish to make it worse as the Rabbi Devashel Torah followed the stricter KMSh Tos in Holin there and the GA there because it is not only from d'rabbanan and KSh to forbid roasting like Shem in Tor, but LL Ds. to divide between prohibition and impurity is not a model of Tos not Sal in the conclusion in the offerings. And evidence of what they said in Hulin Q. 2, Abel Ma'midin, 20, and Kivat Kashira, 20, and Arif, there, and Toss, who wanted to divide between prohibition and impurity, according to his method, which divides there between clear and curd, and the main thing is Keti, who divides between hard and soft, and Km."
  },
  "output/siman_467/peri-megadim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א": "Introduction to siman 467:",
    "1:ב": "(2) First, you need to investigate the C. of the pickle in the pulp when it is a finished sourdough and when it is stiff. And the mark of chametzing in the leaves, and the Omnipresent, what kind of grain are they, and if the leaves are whole, it is finished chametz; whatever has a sign of chametzing or is stiff, it is chametzed. And here in the mass the Sages gave a lesson like the horns of grasshoppers, completely chametzed and obliged to cut off his silver face stiffly. And there is another stiffening of fruit water with water as Basi' 177 and there we interpreted Danoksha, they silvered his face to Didan d'rabbanan, but stiffening of fruit water with water and it is evident in them the acid of Yl Duff for the stiffening hoy mah hoy yash and hm possible y Licha Bia and nam in the past the Passover on him ES Tmz. And it will be seen that most fruit juice with a little water is most water something more than half and a little fruit juice is more than most of them and it has the power to chametz and if it is visible like the horns of grasshoppers it must be cut but fruit water alone without water if it stays all day and its cracks are mixed with each other it is not chametzing them. And Pesachim 18 2 Mebash Mahma liya 20 and we were Afi' within the lesson of a mill in Pusharin per the same name. And it is difficult for a lima cooked in fruit water alone without water, even if water is mixed with it later, there is no mahamah dakbar it is cooked, and the law is a true law even for a friend in the year 1799, the urge is that sheri should be cooked in fruit water, if after mixing water, it does not come into the hands of chametz and whole wheat. And the Rashi who interpreted fruit water alone is stiff. And who will be boiled in fruit water like a mav'd and then mixed with water will see a faded chametz and a proof from siman 60 there Lest we cook well:",
    "1:ג": "(3) And if he was kneading a mill lesson, even though there is no chametz at all, it is a finished chametz and he must cut it, and even though it is not found, yes in P. M.M. He and Minya 5th lesson Mil. And we would have assumed that without a business, he is engaged all day long, and there is no chametz in his business. And that's all there were no signs of Himmutz. Were there any signs of Himmutz? They even silvered his face. Dealing with dough and grasshoppers' horns must be cut. And they probably said per the majority, everyone who deals in nothing comes with Himmutz. Also, the imposition of a cooling sensation on the pulp, as in siman 18, 17, 22 and 11, there is V. He placed in 11 and 11 there the letter 11 and 11. It must be pushrin, as the warmth of the saliva does not cancel the acidification. And here is the imposition of a chill, even all day long, and even above, because if he read stated in the 1st Passover Passover, it is a pleasure to impose a chill until the end of the Shabbat, even if it is cooked as cooked and cooked, it is a mistake to expel it and swallow it as if it were boiled, not for the purpose of pickling, and if one sees a sign of pickling, it does not invalidate the imposition of a chill as stated:",
    "1:ד": "(4) And it is true that all this is in the pulp, but chetim and the the Omnipresent of the kinds of grain that later came with water on them will not be explained explicitly what is their distinct C. Delihoy chametz gomor and must cut them only this alone we found in the words of Chazal Peschim MM A to give chetim cracked are forbidden not cracked are allowed YAS Plogta Deshmuel and Mr. Ukba. And it is true that if there was not born C. a fissure, and the sheaves fell into the water, and there was no lesson Mil Peshita, without a grain, a complete chata from a pulp, KHG Dayin Himmutz, etc. Chilled dough in a 11-year-old ham, and in a masha, a masha is included in all parts of the dough. And it is true that if C. Himmutz was born in the palm of his hand, until the time that he splits he needs to be discussed, both he stayed the lesson Mil when he came to the water, they did not stay the lesson Mil if he was clearly chametzed and must be cut or stiff. Valdidan is a stiff Durbanan. And who is it if there was no split, and the horns stayed a mile when the water came upon them, meaning that there was no chametz at all, and evidence from what Rabbi Ephraim of Arba Dtebeva Bhishta did not ask Raba if they were split, and it was necessary to divide between Minach Neyhi and Latita, p. And it is true that the clothing in section 9 wrote a dish in which wheat and barley were actually cracked, but it is not a complete chametz from the Torah 21, a complete chametz from d'rabbanan, no There is no finished chametz from Durban and APA, they usually forbid the dish YAS from the meaning of Effi. Or to Shmuel and ACP, they were literally split, O you are a complete chametz, and you have to do Kiret MA, even within the lesson of Mil Mishma, etc. And who is the late Haram P.H. from H.M.H.3 and H.H. who wrote Hatims that were not split open? There is no complete chametz. There is no evidence to say whether they were split. The 11th and the 12th, and the name of the HH Damshua, the opinion of the late Rabbi with the Rabbi Rabbi, the late. And AMA Tamaz the the Master is rigid, nullified in the 60th, and so is a sin in the very beginning, and it is forbidden in something, and the friend, who is not softened in any way, is forbidden:",
    "1:ה": "(5) In a Delamidin manner, from this, whole wheats, water came on them and cracked, and there was a lesson of the Mil ACP, Hoy chametz MA, and it is obligatory to cut and the Rosh the Omnipresent Zal Peschim MM 2, Hata in the dough, there is a question of chametz before the lesson Mil or 27, which deals with it anyway, since the pulp is not chametzed, even the wheat from the milk, after the lesson of Mill, actually breaks the chametz of the MA and in the stew, the lesson A mil and be split up really is a MH and must be cut and if there is a mil lesson and we are not split in this, our Rabbi Ephraim is of the opinion to divide between Latita and a minach niyhai AMA 3 4 and 16 3 Dassur and still there is a doubt that a MM is forbidden in the mil lesson and there is no split or a spur and if you imposed a cool down from me or not. Ama letter 12 wrote to Rabbi Ephraim there is no certainty of chametz, but there is doubt of chametz YAS and IA in the interior of this river and the casting of Tsunan A. Batur in the name of Abi Hazri and in the B.I. Shem Darba Dateva Bhishta 11 that was raised from water and the Farah agreed that everything that is placed in water no matter how often from time to time there is no chametz (and we were unleavened) and PZ BHM 27 that are placed in water and not chametzed Even more than 1,100,000 to sell to ACM in the previous Passover that was raised from water. And IA in the SIB will come to Dinah where a whole wheat is found in cool water, it has stayed for a long time without salt and stock and there is found meat DL to allow as a 3 BHM. And Essi' of the Hebrew the Gemara in the second half of the year in the kinds of rifots, Ya'ash:",
    "1:ו": "And one may still question: whence this matter that the Sages said (Rif chapter 39 and Maharam chapter 5:13) a cooked dish with an unsplit grain is permitted — they brought Taz in seif 9 and behold from Pesachim 40a placing or even if stood not like Rabbenu Efrayim, or as Magen Avraham letter 12 wrote this is in water — here in cooking one cannot compare to cold water; and in Pesachim 39b cooked chametzs and does not answer regarding whole grains that did not split — and still this requires study here; and it will be explained in Magen Avraham 12; I will explain further:",
    "1:ז": "(7) Dafoy said that it was not possible to cook the bread with baked chametz and then put it into the flour with fruit water.",
    "1:ח": "And know further that it is fitting to explain the matter of gravy that carries and releases from all of it and in all of it is every liquid thing — water, fruit juice, oil, and dissolved fat; everything cooked in it releases and absorbs throughout. Whence do I derive this? From this I derive it: Yoreh De'ah 104:2 — a mouse in soft fat that moves is like soaked — evidently fat too is called soaked; so too all the more cooked such; and even fat has pulsation — one authority says there is no permitted flavoring for prohibition; and nevertheless we learn everything that is like soaked is like cooked; and from the Torah it forbids until sixty in its non-species. And responsum Beit Yaakov siman 99 implies from his words: when there is a fingerbreadth on it to smear and a grain rests upon it — it is like soaked — requires study; one: Yoreh De'ah 105:61 in Shach 1 what is outside soaking is permitted; and in Taz 64 in gravy itself there is dispute whether it carries what is outside the gravy; and Pesachim 76b: smearing any amount is in the peel; and Yoreh De'ah 105:68 by fire — so it appears; and Magen Avraham 15 in the name of responsum of Rashba 176: a raw sheet like circumcision in salting also 110 degrees — and it will be explained there:",
    "1:ט": "(9) In a moment I'll talk about whole wheat that the sign of cleavage is chamitzin, as if it is found in fruit water, even in the cleavage, one does not say that it has been chamitz before, because from fruit water there is no way through which it can be cleaved, and there is an aleph that is cleaved from fruit water (and if it is cleaved from fruit water, there is no cleaving, and like grasshoppers' horns in the pulp, a sign of sourness in them because a kilo of fruit water is not sour) In branches that were found split, there is a doubt that it was split because of the moisture of the gaddish, or from rainwater that fell on them during the harvest, and the far answer is to depend on the split in the water of the fruit, yes, and so on. Li' to seal and also in a stew with something added to the author of this book and the late Rabbin's Mash, the late Rabbi, there it does not turn out to hang because of the moisture of the Gaddish. We were from the moisture of the Gaddish. And the association of siman 16 and IA in the 16th Yod Abar, in Act A of this, and evidence from the late rabbi's rabbi, Behta in Kou Bai'sa, and here there is no need to extend:",
    "1:י": "(10) And this wheat in honey — even without sixty b'dieved fruit juice does not become chametz — see Levush there and Olat Shabbat. And what is fit to discuss further: chametz — some hold it is \"as if dedicated,\" some hold not — see siman 467 and here and in Levush and Yoreh De'ah 102 Shach 14 and Taz 13; one may say leniently: since for us everything requires burning it is not \"as if dedicated,\" and therefore on last Yom Tov on the eighth day where they practice leniency, and likewise other things permitted to leave overnight — one may say \"as if dedicated,\" and therefore siman 467 Taz note 4 in his section staying permitted and eating forbidden as doubt in \"as if dedicated\" — see Yoreh De'ah Shach 57 from this and here in 462:11 to permit on doubt to eat for time of need, and impossible to wait on a hen then important doubt. Know: doubt in \"as if dedicated\" in any amount — examine in Shach there note 57 implies somewhat the reason that anything in a thousand is not nullified — likewise in doubt; one may say see there note 56. Nevertheless all we are stringent due to custom — if still doubt one may permit like doubt of prepared on second Yom Tov — see inside explained. Further one may say mixtures before Pesach siman 462:2 permitted to leave overnight from doubt and forbidden to eat — unlike made during Pesach since they practice to burn everything and forbidden to leave — we do not distinguish to permit on doubt leaving and forbid eating like 462 in plucking chickens and this son.",
    "1:כ": "We hold in Yoreh De'ah that a thing forbidden in any amount — we do not say in it: flavor is not flavor; practical difference for us: a pot with cooked dish and a split grain found — everything forbidden; and afterward Avi HaEzri placed it in an oven — this pot Avi HaEzri with dish and other pots — one might say it is aroma in any amount and purging permits — see Magen Avraham 447 letter 41 and it will be explained in Taz letter 17 in two any-amounts I will explain this:",
    "1:ל": "In salting — see seif 14 and it will be explained there whether eaten because of its saltiness or not; and brine from salted meat even if eaten because of saltiness is considered boiling as in siman 91:65 in Yoreh De'ah. And if it fell on a vessel — it will be explained there there's help. And if he left meat in a vessel that had a grain in brine, or left meat there and poured boiling water on them — see Yoreh De'ah 95 Shach letter 24 — and it will be explained below seif 14 from this; and here one cannot expand:",
    "1:מ": "Or Taz and Magen Avraham who do not follow Orach Chayyim Shabbos who is concerned lest the gentile grind and sell to an Israelite — so too the Ran for his view that splitting is not permitted in grains at all placing — and he establishes in split ones unlike for us like Rabbenu Efrayim in seif 2 resting at ease; and so Rashi Pesachim 40a: these grains that are not recognizable, etc. — for if recognizable he would not challenge from kilayim to perhaps he will grind. And behold the Tur doubled his words and there is concern for chametzing and it is not recognizable — one does not sell to a gentile lest he sell to an Israelite; since chametzing is not recognizable — so in Levush; and it should have said grains that sank — one does not sell to a gentile if chametzing is not recognizable, only at the beginning a leniency for an Israelite permitted without informing even if not recognizable; and for a gentile forbidden when not recognizable — behold if recognizable permitted for a gentile too; and one might say at first since not recognizable there is concern he will sell thus; but to be concerned he will grind and sell all this — not in concern for chametzing; also for Rabbenu Efrayim there is no certain chametz only safek and perhaps only stiff — Magen Avraham 12 and letter 4 — except concern he will sell thus since not recognizable; and if they split open obviously forbidden for it is d'oraisa and we are concerned he will grind; leniency at beginning and end; and end of siman and Ateret Zekenim 1; and for what Chayyei Adam wrote: transgressing Beit Yosef — Rabbenu Efrayim there: nullified because not seen — see there. And end of siman 2: if he transgressed and kept and sowed after Pesach the shoots are permitted 463 letter 9 — requires study without splitting for it is only concern for chametzing and safek whether stood measure of a mil — Magen Avraham 4; and perhaps stiff is all that did not split open at all — Magen Avraham 12; if so safek chametz after Pesach is d'rabbanan — see siman 448-449 and we wrote there from this. And do not err to explain concern for chametzing as meaning concern he will sell to an Israelite — that stretch cannot be said; only concern for chametzing, not full chametz as stated. And the Mechaber who omitted that it is not recognizable — it implies for a gentile in every case forbidden lest he grind flour; and for an Israelite a little is obvious permitted when recognizable; and 448 Taz and Magen Avraham agreed to permit when splitting recognizable even much to a gentile; and Magen Avraham 1-2 and it will be explained there further:",
    "2:_": "And we inform you: see Turei Zahav Pesachim 40b and Maharam chapter 5:19 and as written here in Shulchan Aruch. And the Taz understood from the simple sense of Maharshal that he explained the words of Maharam thus in his version: keva keva stam — meaning both for Israelite and gentile; and for Israelite with informing and for gentile without informing because there is safek sefeika with a gentile unlike Israelite on the contrary there; there the prohibition for an Israelite is also with informing because perhaps a stumbling block will come from not informing — therefore they did not mention in the Gemara permission of much with informing; and on this the Taz questioned: certainly Maharam and the Mechaber hold specifically for a gentile a little; for an Israelite whether much or little with informing permitted specifically; and the Tur who wrote and it is reasonable like the author of the Ittur for his view that reads keva for Israelite. Avi HaEzri. However for halachah he agreed to Maharshal and as the Tur's version: for Israelite keva and with informing specifically, for we do not rely that he will eat before Pesach — for in presumption and majority too we do not rely wherever it can be fixed easily like that of R' Papa: ask him — behold much with informing not lest he forget to inform; and for a gentile little permitted because safek sefeika perhaps he will not sell to Israelite and perhaps Israelite will eat before Pesach; even l'chatchila one does not make safek sefeika in one's hands — this is preferable that presumably he will eat for his need. And there is a corruption in Taz — it should read: unlike when selling little to a gentile there is safek sefeika; and for Israelite much and specifically little with informing; and Bach seif 2 stringent as it appears for gentile little forbidden and for Israelite little with informing; and Netziv permits little to gentile as safek sefeika; and on the contrary — and it will be explained in Magen Avraham:",
    "3:_": "For since Taz and Magen Avraham 4 brought the long Mordechai and it will be explained there; and behold what the Taz wrote to benefit from them together — meaning two by two not all together — see Yoreh De'ah 110:67; however in safek prohibition that became mixed and one fell to the sea one might say all permitted together too as safek sefeika perhaps there is no prohibition and perhaps the prohibition fell to the sea — see there and here in Magen Avraham. And what he wrote there is a leap in the Agur — Bach questioned thus; that Mordechai deals with recognizable splitting unlike here where those upon which rain fell remove them and the rest have safek sefeika; and he explained there: the question is as there permission if eating some is safek sefeika — so here in the middle of the grains there is safek sefeika all that removes the upper ones; and what the Taz wrote in any amount d'rabbanan requires study — those that became moist remove them and the rest safek whether rain fell on all or not. Also what he wrote in 453 if grinding permitted because does not return and revive — flour wet in wet; as Magen Avraham wrote there 6 and Taz there 2; and why here rule returns and revives, etc. — meaning why forbid eating too — requires study; and to say the difficulty is why take those that became moist and grind all before Pesach like grain that sprouted where there is sixty — there is effort to clarify and also b'dieved as there; unlike here where one should take those that became moist — one might say presumably rain did not fall on all the grain but safek perhaps fell on some and dried — for this he said if certainly there are sixty against safek that perhaps rain fell on some it is obvious permitted for eating and to grind before Pesach; and therefore safek — still difficulty: since it is safek why not permit eating like every safek sefeika; and the language is somewhat garbled; and what he wrote as there if sixty the reason is not to strengthen prohibition — we do not strengthen from safek sefeika but it deals presumptive prohibition and as there permitting eating from safek sefeika and here forbidding eating; and Magen Avraham 5 and Taz 5 and Peri Chadash here and in Darkei Moshe Florence edition I saw in gloss: Agur's law does not contradict Rashba's law — namely stacks are in the stalk and hard and upper parts of the stack like a roof and covering and recognizable rain did not reach the middle of the stack — see there:",
    "4:א": "However from responsum of the Ran siman 59 brought in Shulchan Aruch seif 6 there is difficulty — except there too it implies the reason like Rashba's responsum that it is common when resting on sheaves rain drips on the upper parts of the stack and in the middle water does not reach — unlike the Agur incident of grain in an attic where there is safek in all of it — he properly said not to rely to permit for eating. And what the Rambam wrote in seif 12 to permit through safek sefeika — one might say in any amount d'rabbanan; but to eat grains in their substance not; and further one might say safek sefeika born on Pesach permits safek sefeika, for for us chametz is not called davar sheyeish lo matirin — if we forbid we must burn everything and to permit retaining and forbid eating is not seen; therefore we are lenient in everything unlike before Pesach where davar sheyeish lo matirin is such as to permit retaining and not eating — we wrote this in the introduction. And for halachah the Taz wrote in pressing circumstances permitted; and Chayyei Adam 10 and Magen Avraham 2 questioned two by two; and here concern perhaps rain fell on all; and see Orach Chayyim Shabbos what he wrote that safek sefeika like majority and chametz in any amount they did not follow majority; and until nightfall safek sefeika requires distinction between that of seif 12 — see there; and safek sefeika that reverses — see Yoreh De'ah 110 on this and it will be explained in letter 5 on this:",
    "4:ב": "And indeed from responsum of the Ran siman 59 it is difficult — except there too it implies the reason like Rashba that it is common in sheaves that rain drips on upper parts of the stack and in the middle water does not reach — unlike in the Agur incident. And what the Mechaber wrote in seif 3 if their appearance did not change — behold if changed forbidden — Avi HaEzri and Levush also forbidden to retain them — behold they did not come in a ship; even if appearance changed if there are no others one might say permitted:",
    "5:א": "(1) There is no 17th of the late Rabbinical Rabbi in the answer mark 12 and in the letter 4 we wrote in the name of the DM here it is obvious to the eye that the rain did not rule through the medium of the Gaddish and also in the 7th of the Rabbin's answer it is found that the rain will not pass through the medium of the Gaddish and in the Rabbinate there it is covered with oats and straw above and indeed it is necessary to look at why the author brought in the Rabbinic answer of the Rabbinate of that year that there is no fear for just a goat of that year, i.e. Ruba and KMS MA H, and the author did not write the sipa afi, midway the Gaddish, and there was no need to copy the Rabbin's answer in the SO because it is she and who is that DSO copied to say Delionot that the chametz is broken by chametz, and they will not be touched by moisture that has discolored fruit water because the L.S. Doubtful and certainly chametz YAS. And M. M. M. S. B. S. D. that there is no fear of just the sheaves of that year being hard, and the rest of the piles of that year are permitted. Undoubtedly and needs to be looked into for Dina:",
    "5:ב": "And what he wrote regarding saffron — at first since we see saffron dissolved in the cooked dish and nothing remains — necessarily even if nevelah meat threads are mixed custom is to invert and all becomes like saffron — therefore we follow present state; somewhat strained to explain Taz's words; also there is concern Yom Tov — see there; and Magen Avraham letter 3 and Chayyei Adam here letter 16 wrote novel fat that did not change to a permitted thing — nevertheless one might say we do not permit forbidden fat for it is not such a change as blood which the Torah forbade and did not change to another appearance — and blood that was cooked is permitted for most codifiers and so is the halachah unlike forbidden fat; no verse needed — see there. And if known flour or fine flour was put in honey and fell into a dish in less than sixty — Chayyei Adam relying on leniency of Rabbenu Yonah that it turned to honey did not lose — see there and Magen Avraham letter 11 — meaning when it stood some time in the honey — for in short time it does not turn to honey:",
    "6:א": "And know in Yoreh De'ah 84 and Shach letter 37 honey is accustomed to preserve and sustain things hidden in it; as Rashba wrote in responsum 80: whole things preserve; like Bava Batra regarding Herod who hid seven years in honey; and cut things quickly consumed — see there; therefore whole grains found in honey on Pesach even though long time — soaked preserves; and soaked in soft honey honey becomes chametz and forbids the dish in any amount — no distinction whether there was living soul in it or one grain unlike fine flour long time turned to honey — permitted b'dieved as written. And it will be explained in Magen Avraham 7:",
    "6:ב": "And figs — Taz wrote flour — one might say fruit juice and fine flour concern — see Levush; and in my humble opinion zafarin too forbidden lest substitution; and see Levush: honey from a barrel is common and we are not concerned for substitution unlike zafarin in my view not common — they say it came from overseas; and so dried fruits from Israel people buy known and common to dry for Pesach and no substitution. And fruit a gentile dried with chametz bread in one oven — from aroma one might say b'dieved forbid the mixture — we hold bread aroma does not forbid — see siman 447; nevertheless one might say touched chametz bread. And raisins red — not eaten; small black ones some permit — see there and Magen Avraham letter 8 and it will be explained there:",
    "6:ג": "And know in honey and wine if it stood long — one might say split from wine and honey and permitted unlike frying in fat which sense testifies splitting does not occur from fat frying — if found split in the rounds one might say common and attribute from elsewhere; but in softened alone we permit for us — as stated; and it will be explained letter 12; and in Mishnah Berurah regarding roasted chicken with grain found — also:",
    "7:_": "And figs — Taz wrote flour; fruit juice and fine flour concern; Levush; zafarin forbidden lest substitution; dried fruit from Israel not substituted:",
    "8:א": "And tsuker kandil — Taz wrote in sour vats; stam vessels Avi HaEzri and Yom Tov against Yom Tov is from siman 447 from this; and end of siman; and what tsuker Candia they eat on last Yom Tov — hot tsuker without real cooking; experts cook in special vessels — product is cane grown in ground boiled in water extracted like flour — so merchants said; Magen Avraham letter 9; l'chatchila practice to sell even hot tsuker; b'dieved not forbidden; even our kandil cooked in sour vats if stood — not forbidden after Pesach for nevelah ben nevelah; Avi HaEzri siman 447 there. And Taz: not common among us — properly concerned for substitution like figs from his garden; and in my view zafarin letter 9 and 7 — recognizable distinct form:",
    "8:ב": "And if pressing circumstances on Pesach market day and much selling tsuker — one might say permitted to sell hot tsuker on pressing circumstances; merchants testified no flour mixed in hot tsuker — everything by custom; where practice prohibition do not permit before them; where practice permission permitted. Now Israelites make hot tsuker for Pesach with some change lest substitution — well done and fixed per great ones of the generation and Peri Chadash. See Levush: some lenient in kandil tsuker; on last Yom Tov do not be so stringent — see there:",
    "9:_": "Saffron — Taz and Levush; see letter 7 distinction between common here and not common — concerned:",
    "10:א": "Even Taz in the name of Bach there in seif 11 that softened — at least require; reason some stringent concern to rule like Mar Ukva Pesachim 40a all that placed on opening of roaster and would split — we are not expert; but not softened hard ones we are expert — no chametzing reached — see there and Peri Chadash here. And it appears to me: only when there are sixty — any amount d'rabbanan; but without sixty like eating grains in their substance — Torah safek for all who say d'oraisa; and in seif 2 grain sunk in river though not split forbidden — Rava did not ask splitting — also did not ask eating in their substance forbidden Torah safek; all the more in hot cooked dish hard kernels not permitted even without sixty — Magen Avraham letter 12. However if grain fell into dish above boiling — hilat — see 454 Magen Avraham 4: if very hard even without sixty lenient — ruling authority must be careful. And regarding benefit: softened not split — Taz letter 15 implies forbidden in one safek for benefit burn all; Ateret Zekenim letter 18 agreed forbidden for benefit; Eshel Avraham letter 26 leaned b'dieved to permit benefit — requires study:",
    "10:ב": "An incident occurred in our community K'K Kamerani: they made round loaves called knaidlach from ground matzah kneaded with goose fat and salt and pepper without any water and fried in fat rising on all sides; afterward placed in a bowl and poured boiling gravy from kli rishon; found in one a softened grain; they asked me the law of the food and vessels on Pesach:",
    "10:ג": "(3) Answer: When it softens alone and the wheat does not split, the food will be considered delicious. We guess that fruit water with eggs does not chametz and a little salt, even in our cooking, since it changes its shape, there is no chametz in juice. Ya'ash and 1777 during the pressure of Ya'ash and flaflin without water, one does not miss Esi' 25 in Ma'a 19. And 100% in salt, blood is important. And what caused the sauce, the sauce wouldn't reach the middle of the circles at all, and the infusion of KC works even for the MD with KS sourdough, Nami Osar MM was not soured in the slightest moment by the sauce that did not reach into the circles, and it is enough to throw away the bread that the bread found in it, not the rest. And if they find chaffed wheat in the A. of them, one should discuss and say that the split C. pickles and fruit juice are not from sourness 11 of the preceding, the chamitz is even deficient from the hexkinen, it is forbidden as the 3rd, the contrary to her assumptions, and who is from the author in 1776, hetta with honey is not prohibited, etc. There are no sixes in honey, AMA 12 and refer to the commentary on Zemach Tzedek, C. 12, and there is nothing that prevents it from hatching in fruit water and in egg water, and it is possible for the pulp to be like grasshoppers' horns, and more in fruit water and APA there is no chametz, and in water, this is C. Himmutz. And here in the SIA, the rabbi decided to forbid the mass, even without a rule. Before leaving the chametz and we were discussing that it is possible to split the wheat, there is no clear sign, but when the split chametz is complete, it is the same in fruit water. And A'ran in response was brought in siman 19 here 5 (in siman 19) DLSH that it will be hatched from the moisture of the Gaddish, we were a damet, it is a spring that is kept in the days of many fruits, it is hatched and indeed the spring in the Parach here, section 16 of the act of siman 16, is eaten in goose fat. He will testify that there is a way to break out because of the frying of the fat, and if he finds in the circles a hen in siman 11, he will always be with me, but when she is softened alone, she will be left alone, and as mentioned, and in the letter of the 12th, there will be another, and in the 15th, on a roasted hen that he will find a hen on.",
    "10:ד": "(4) If they gave the circles in a bowl and cut and stirred a sauce and found a softened ham, there is no limit for prohibiting infusion and a stricter without there being a mill rate in the broth. And even if it is already cooked in fat, lest it was not cooked well, etc. If they cut the wheat and found white flour, then we saw that it was not cooked well, and it was not cooked well. ובהקדמה הקשינו מפסחים ל״ט ב׳ מבושל מחמע ולא מוקי במי פירות תו אח״כ במים אין מחמיץ ולרש״י דנוקשה הוי שפיר ועשו״ת הר״ן נון ט׳ י״ל כה״ג הוי מי פירות ומים וי״ל נוקשה בטל בס׳ ואי״ה באות כ״א יבואר בזה:",
    "11:א": "On Yom Tov — Taz for Mechaber in 447:10 permitted; for us custom to forbid — obvious permitted to retain on eighth all the more from any amount. But less than sixty forbidden to retain — Magen Avraham 14. General rule: all permitted to retain — permitted to sell and benefit; but some forbidden to retain permitted to sell as Mechaber wrote; see Levush here. And since Taz did not learn only a fortiori — implies other two d'rabbanan like stiff less than sixty (Magen Avraham 447:5, Taz 19) or dry in dry in its species in majority or species in wet less than sixty; and imagine burned Pesach wine with chametz wine (though essence not its species we follow taste for leniency and stringency — see 98 Shach 6; until nightfall wine for its taste and smells in thousand — decree unlike not its species) — forbidden to retain on eighth of Pesach; and what more from any amount one might rely on questions, etc. However Levush wrote last Yom Tov d'rabbanan and retaining also d'rabbanan — two d'rabbanan they were not stringent — implies every two d'rabbanan do not combine in retaining. However other days even two d'rabbanan purging and any amount or other two d'rabbanan — do not learn from eighth of Pesach; see 447 Magen Avraham letter 41. And know prohibition of retaining in half measure is still stiff; all the more in wet in its non-species less than sixty that reverted to prohibition — palate tastes and tastes — for all perhaps still stiff; Peri Chadash here and Magen Avraham 447:42 and 44. And if nullified by mouth and said all chametz I will have on Pesach is nullified — for those who can nullify even what did not yet exist — see 446 Magen Avraham 1 and responsum of Moreinu from Lubin and responsum Bach; nevertheless if less than sixty on eighth in its non-species forbidden to retain for retaining d'rabbanan — nevertheless if not nullified d'oraisa also for Rashbatz in Maamar Yavin Shemuah and so Maharan beginning retaining stiff because of eating prohibition though stiff nullified suffices — nevertheless close to Torah prohibition. And it will be explained Magen Avraham 18:",
    "11:ב": "And know roasted by fire forbidden from chametz — so it appears — see Yoreh De'ah 105 distinctions: where sixty required by law no removal needed; where stringency required sixty and by law amount of removal — on eighth such forbidden to retain must remove as it appears and discard — rest permitted to retain. And if nullified by mouth until nightfall forbidden as it appears; and afterward cooked in pot before nightfall even if sixty in piece against prohibition — as it appears we say flavor is not flavor — see 96 Shach 9 — then needs sixty against removal; forbidden for purging does not apply to food — see 106 there Taz and Shach 1-2. Therefore piece forbidden by law on eighth by amount of removal — transgressed and retained after Pesach and cooked in kli rishon afterward — all without sixty against removal — depends on dispute 447 Magen Avraham 44 whether after Pesach needs sixty or less than sixty also not acquired. There we wrote one might say only nevelah through d'rabbanan transgressed — therefore for all no sixty needed against absorption; perhaps permitted reverted to prohibition — same for Beit Yosef; perhaps Beit Yosef no palate tastes. Understand this — requires study; end of siman I will write more laws scattered to this; in opening beginning of book part 3 we expanded laws pertaining to this siman:",
    "11:ג": "And know on eighth of Pesach in complete chametz or without sixty — even though did not transgress stiff Beit Yosef for we are expert in fixed month — nevertheless R' Shimon acquires after Pesach as explained Tur 448 bread chametz gift to Israelite — two days equal lest come to treat lightly — see 448. And even if he made dough on eighth of Pesach and saw no time to bake unless it chametzed first and preceded and nullified and transgressed afterward and retained — we do not say two d'rabbanan such as written; Magen Avraham 447 letter 46. And what was questioned how burn on Yom Tov and also seventh of Pesach how burn — see 446 Magen Avraham letter 2 and responsum Chatam Sofer; Magen Avraham 14 will explain; responsum Penei Yehoshua part 1:15; also Magen Avraham 14 there's help:",
    "12:_": "As well as the 17th of his holy words, we need an explanation that the late Rabbi Pesachim, page M. Aha Dantbka'u 20 wrote a chatta in the matzah 11 DAC to take that the chametz is not finished only close to the 20 and since the dough was not chametzed as the chata and they did not feel that the chata had been in water that was too small to keep the water. And some forbade that unleavened bread and some forbade making sacrifices with it, and we will not be afraid, as well as the Ram's writing, Dehotach, a place where you can eat bread. And so his conclusion is to say that if you doubt chametz, you don't need it, and the Ram clearly says that sour wheat is sufficient for roasting and baking. In the opinion of the deceased, nothing is prohibited in the pulp. And the hard part 2 of the Rash's Dhamim keep it from the meaning of the word of the Omnipresent Hata sour forbids all water and Lekman wrote chametz in cold water there is no prohibition of dayin NT in the cold (brought in the SIB there are those who forbid it during Pesach) and T. Dakan during Pesach Shafir said if there was anything in the water that escaped and forbids every third of water YAS and here the author here brought the words of the Rash to the Halacha Hata Bekoa In kneading or baking, as in the material, even in the dough or from the dough, the kneading presses the place where the wheat is affected, it emits the wheat in the dough and nothing more, because water preserves it, and if it fell during the kneading or in the flour, there was wheat, and for a short time when the water stirred the dough, they did not feel that the water had soured, only in the kneading, the wheat wound was enough to take. And there are those who forbid all the dough or the same matzah apparently It is the answer of the Rashba, the late Kevu, brought in 2 and there forbids the whole dough, not the matzah only, from the reasons A, that the matzah touched through rolling all the dough (and in the cold Nami because of the pressure of the kneading, something emits) and also that the water brings out the flavor in every Yas, and it is said that there are those who forbid the whole dough, the Rashba, and Tor on behalf of others, all the matzah (and not the baked ones only from what they soaked in water ABH) or the same The unleavened bread called a little large and we were here were found and here he was in Ariba and through his incarnation he touched all the dough in his hand that unleavened bread and Ama 15 and looked at the well of the exile and the son. And the rabbi who corrected during the Passover and before the Passover is not apparently the intention of the 16th in which during the Passover they felt slaughtered in cold water and something was thrown out and forbids all the dough and before the Passover something is void and KMSh in the 12th and the 16th the letter 15 but still what difference is there between a dough or a matzah and even a question in this from the Rabbi's words the commentator who relied on the answer of the late Rabbi And KMS MA 15 VI 15 Dam found the matzah in his hand here, this matzah fell and is forbidden in the cold during Pesach De'i Mi'uch and Dikha something emits in the cold and if it is in a complete mass the whole mass is forbidden and this is during Pesach in something that precedes Pesach voids something that is emitted in Shishim and Adam. And the 16th Mash to explain the Rabbi's words ABH and a requirement and clothing, etc. in the DM where the Rabbi says that goat is forbidden in the sunanin and the water is forbidden (on the contrary, the opinion of Avi Hazri was brought in the column and explained in the 12b) and so Shafir said that if it was in the water the goat was soured and then by kneading it is forbidden in the coolness of the dough Damsho Flit in the third And look at the letter 15. And in the 16th edition, Rama ruled According to Maharil and Harsh we have already written about this above. And the MS was softened even though it was not split into Didan and the Rabbi who did not proofread BSI and A and B and CD relied on the Amsh in the ST and so on and so on Y. L. Dashri in eating and eating and enjoying Y. L. A. F. Laman will be pushed after I B.S. And the letter 17 was written by the apothecary YASH. A dam is found in the water and it is doubtful that it was above the sea. And M. in the name of the clothing that the Hacha Dam Amarinan just fell did not miss at all Dam the pulp is not apparent Himmutz KS Hata the hard and the 16th Dashma has already missed R.L. The division of the Iglai Mountains is a division, per the majority, and also Kirt Kms in the introduction, as it was necessarily sour before, and the 16th M. is doubtless sour, per the method of the late Rabbi who sided with Dahta in the Koea, a chametz doubt is, and Ksh. And the PFZ softened to the didan in the SAT DAA in Kayin 11 now it fell like the difficulty of clothing. And if in the dough you don't see C. Himmutz, it is sour and it is proof that the sourness was already softened by itself, if not baked 11 and 14. And Am'sh comes with the letter Yo'd in the act of Decamerna and Asi' 17th 20th and 15th in Goose fat. And Afarah Stu in this:",
    "13:א": "If Taz: see seif 8:11 Shach 27 and 105 Taz 4 all the more forbid without pressing circumstances for it purges and absorbs unlike together; siman 447 Magen Avraham 9; 454 letter 3 and 13 see there; chametz in any amount forbids more; one hard grain to chicken if Yom Tov in water; before Pesach equals other prohibitions; this plucking — though Yoreh De'ah 88:11 forbids l'chatchila in any amount all the more here salted on feathers rinsed plucked in any amount:",
    "13:ב": "Avi HaEzri end of siman brought Mordechai: plucked chicken on mouth of chametz vessel — forbade chicken — see there; Chayyei Adam letter 33; indeed until nightfall in any amount should permit; pouring only 110 degrees until nightfall; perhaps this authority until nightfall in any amount even for those who purify and absorb unlike Ran; Magen Avraham 454:3; Chayyei Adam 8; even Beit Yosef permit; all the more Avi HaEzri; Eshel Avraham; Eshel Avraham 14:95 letter 24:",
    "14:א": "(1) אסורה עט״ז עמ״א אות י״ו וח״י וכ״א מזמן לזמן מחזיקין איסור וממקום למקום לא מחזיקין איסור. And in the column of an act that was read by Rabbi Rabin Yash and forbade in the Ku'ah and the author did not mention its errors in the Set. Indeed in the Ku'ah, was the stew softened even in the Khar in something. It is permissible and the meaning is before boiling, before boiling the chicken (not in Tsonnin and from place to place they do not hold a prohibition). Likewise, the words of the 16th must be reconciled with the words of the MA to divide between time and place and refer to the letter 15, 17 and 18 there More will come. And the text of the 16th and one should not make it difficult for the Hoys (TS in some patterns) lest it now fall and be digested by a DL hen in whole wheat even in an undigested hatch. And from M.S. that it was not left badly soured, the author wrote, and it is certain that chametz Ladidan and K.M. in the letter 15. And it is possible to sell to a didan, even if it is softened, it is forbidden in the League of Mothers, Hoys SS, and T. Despeck, a lack of wisdom is not within the bounds of doubt.",
    "14:ב": "If softened alone not split — food permitted; fruit juice with eggs; little salt form changed; 462:6; 463:1 b'dieved; 462 pressing; pepper; gravy did not reach middle; 110 degrees; discard one bread; split — fruit juice; 462:6; 467:9; knaidlach; Rosh; Ran; Peri Chadash; letter 12:",
    "14:ג": "Further Mechaber lenient softened in any amount one safek d'rabbanan lenient; here one safek stringent; perhaps time to time worse establish on presumption lack of knowledge in rabbinic safek lenient Yoreh De'ah 105 safek soaked; Kaf HaChayyim Shach 1 in any amount perhaps we say it is different; therefore same for other meat:",
    "15:_": "Split grain Taz for Mechaber seif 9 even one safek in dish in any amount without split dish permitted Magen Avraham 12 all the more safek sefeika; except Rambam agrees here one safek forbids benefit not only softened unlike Chayyei Adam letter 10; split grain for us complete chametz no safek unlike Rosh Pesachim split grain; see seif 11 Peri Chadash end Magen Avraham:",
    "16:_": "If Taz as letter 12 cold does not purge and absorb at all even any amount; Rosh waters guard else grain sour forbids water; appears his view; see 17; Levush 11-12; examiner Tur Bach sees Avi HaEzri disputes Tur: sour grain in cold waters nogeia possible even cold; Tur reverse: grain sour in cold waters no taste in water; Avi HaEzri there split, etc.; Mechaber split grain or chametz bread permitted during Pesach — Tur view even Pesach no purging in cold; some forbid — Rosh waters guard Taz 12; if before Pesach and who is anonymous permitting even during; Avi HaEzri did not permit chametz bread anyway forbids; Beer HaGolah letter 70 requires study; Magen Avraham 19 will explain:"
  }
};
